HTML QUICK SUMMARY
===================
WHAT IS HTML?
-------------
HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language. It is the standard markup
language used to create web pages. HTML is NOT a programming language;
it is a markup language that describes the structure and content of a
web page using elements.

WHY IS HTML IMPORTANT?
--------------------------------------
- It is the foundation of every website on the internet.
- It defines the structure and content of a web page (text, images,
  links, forms, tables, etc.).
- It works together with CSS (styling) and JavaScript (behavior) to
  build complete, interactive websites.
- It is easy to learn,free, and supported by every web browser.
- Semantic HTML improves accessibility and SEO (helps search engines
  understand the page content).
- It allows browsers to build the DOM (Document Object Model), which
  can be manipulated using JavaScript to create dynamic pages.
MOST IMPORTANT CONCEPTS IN HTML
--------------------------------
1. HTML Document Structure:
   <!DOCTYPE html>, <html>, <head>, <body>

2. Elements & Tags:
   - Elements usually have an opening tag, content, and a closing tag:
     <p>This is a paragraph</p>
   - Some elements are self-closing (void elements): <img>, <br>, <input>
   - Elements can have attributes (name="value") that add extra info,
     e.g. <p id="myId" class="intro">...</p>

3. Block vs Inline Elements:
   - Block-level elements start on a new line and take full width
     ( <div>, <p>, <h1>-<h6>, <ul>, <form>).
   - Inline elements flow within the line (<span>, <a>, <b>, <img>).

4. Structural vs Semantic Elements:
   - Structural (no meaning): <div>, <span>
   - Semantic (meaningful): <header>, <nav>, <main>, <section>,
     <article>, <aside>, <footer>, <figure>
   Semantic elements make code more readable and improve SEO/accessibility.

5. The DOM (Document Object Model):
   - A tree-like representation of the HTML document that allows
     JavaScript to access, modify, or manipulate page content and style.

6. Comments:
   - Written as: <!-- This is a comment -->
   - Not shown in the browser, used to explain code or disable sections.

MOST COMMONLY USED HTML TAGS
------------------------------
Document structure:
  <html>, <head>, <title>, <body>, <meta>, <link>, <script>, <style>

Text content:
  <h1> to <h6>  -> Headings
  <p>           -> Paragraph
  <br>          -> Line break
  <hr>          -> Horizontal rule
  <strong> / <b> -> Bold text
  <em> / <i>     -> Italic text
  <span>        -> Inline generic container

Links & media:
  <a href="...">  -> Hyperlink
  <img src="...">  -> Image
  <video>, <audio> -> Media

Lists:
  <ul>  -> Unordered list
  <ol>  -> Ordered list
  <li>  -> List item

Tables:
  <table>, <tr>, <th>, <td>, <thead>, <tbody>

Forms:
  <form>, <input>, <label>, <textarea>, <select>, <option>,
  <button>, <fieldset>, <legend>

Layout / structure (semantic):
  <div>, <header>, <nav>, <main>, <section>, <article>,
  <aside>, <footer>, <figure>

QUICK NOTES
------------
- HTML5 is the latest major version and introduced new elements
  (semantic tags, <audio>, <video>, <canvas>) and new input types.
- Always structure your page properly: DOCTYPE > html > head/body.
- Use semantic tags whenever possible instead of only <div>/<span>.
- Every attribute value should be inside quotes: attribute="value".
- HTML works hand-in-hand with CSS (styling) and JavaScript (behavior).